# Use another model provider

This project calls Claude through the Anthropic API. Moving to another
provider's model changes a few files, and it can change what happens to the
messages your users send. Read the second part before the first.

## What changes in the code

- [`src/{{ package_name }}/claude.py`](../../src/{{ package_name }}/claude.py):
  the client `make_client()` makes, `MODEL`, and the call in `ask()`. Keep
  the shape: the client made in that one place and passed in, the model named
  once, the prompt read from `prompts/system.md`, and no message text in the
  logs.
- The dependency: `uv add` the provider's SDK, then `uv remove anthropic`.
- [`tests/conftest.py`](../../tests/conftest.py): refuse the new provider's
  client in tests the same way, so the tests and CI still need no key.
- [`tests/test_claude.py`](../../tests/test_claude.py): the fake answers in
  the new provider's shape.
- `.env.example` and the host's secrets: the new provider's key name.
- README.md, "Calls Claude": the key, its spend limit, what the provider
  keeps, and the notice for your users are Anthropic's. Write them again for
  the new provider from its own terms.

`.copier-answers.yml` still says `calls_claude: true`, so a template update
keeps updating these files. Expect to resolve the differences by hand.

## What changes in the terms: Google Gemini's unpaid tier

Google's Gemini API has an unpaid tier, and its terms differ from Anthropic's
API terms (the [Gemini API terms](https://aistudio.google.com/docs/terms),
read 2026-11-28). On the unpaid tier:

- Google uses what you send and the answers to provide and improve its
  products: your inputs may be used to improve products.
- Human reviewers may read, annotate and process your inputs and outputs.
- Personal information must not be sent: the terms say not to submit
  sensitive, confidential or personal information.
- It may not serve users in the European Economic Area, the UK or
  Switzerland: only the paid services may be offered to them.

So the unpaid tier suits trying a model on text you made up. Once real users
type into the project, the notice in the README is no longer true there; use
a paid tier, and read its terms first.
